267
00:11:32,080 --> 00:11:40,440
Okay, so this is a really important question also because in your application, you have to put how long you plan to stay in the US.

268
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:41,440
Right.

269
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:42,450
It's a pretty straightforward answer.

270
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:44,990
You know, two months, one month, three weeks.

271
00:11:45,520 --> 00:11:46,520
Right.

272
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:50,570
But you just have to make sure that your answer matches what's written in your application form.

273
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:52,480
Yeah, exactly.

274
00:11:52,480 --> 00:11:54,250
Okay, now let's take a look at our last question.

275
00:11:56,040 --> 00:11:58,360
Tell me about the ties you have to your home country.

276
00:12:00,660 --> 00:12:02,980
Tell me about the ties you have to your home country.

277
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:08,340
Well, I think this is maybe the most important question that they'll ask you, right?

278
00:12:08,760 --> 00:12:10,480
Yeah, this is the most important question.

279
00:12:10,720 --> 00:12:13,160
This is what the interviewer is most interested in.

280
00:12:13,280 --> 00:12:13,540
Okay.

281
00:12:13,740 --> 00:12:15,800
So why is he interested in this?

282
00:12:16,280 --> 00:12:23,220
Because he has to be sure that you will not become an illegal immigrant to the United States.

283
00:12:23,420 --> 00:12:25,700
So that you will return to your home country.

284
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:27,000
Exactly.

285
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,540
He or she has to be convinced that you're going to return to your home country.

286
00:12:30,240 --> 00:12:31,460
So how can you convince him?

287
00:12:31,840 --> 00:12:33,350
Basically, by demonstrating your ties.

288
00:12:33,730 --> 00:12:36,850
So showing the things that will pull you back to your home country.

289
00:12:37,210 --> 00:12:43,630
So if you take documents to support this, bank statements, maybe a copy of your mortgage.

290
00:12:43,720 --> 00:12:46,240
Yeah, like maybe your wedding certificate.

291
00:12:47,210 --> 00:12:50,130
I don't know, even a letter from your office, from your job.

292
00:12:50,630 --> 00:12:53,290
So that shows that you have to return to your work.

293
00:12:53,630 --> 00:12:57,970
The more things you bring, the better, even though the interviewer might not ask for the documents.

294
00:12:58,680 --> 00:13:00,680
Okay, it's just better to be safe, right?

295
00:13:01,080 --> 00:13:02,080
Exactly.

296
00:13:02,080 --> 00:13:03,960
So come with a huge file full of things.

297
00:13:03,960 --> 00:13:04,960
Exactly.

298
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:10,260
I've had friends who've arrived to the embassy with a huge file of documents and weren't asked to show any of the documents.

299
00:13:11,320 --> 00:13:12,960
But other interviewers will maybe ask for it.

300
00:13:13,200 --> 00:13:15,320
Okay, so now let's listen to the dialogue again.

301
00:13:15,600 --> 00:13:17,940
And you'll hear how our traveler answers these questions.

302
00:13:28,450 --> 00:13:30,430
So you're applying for a B2 visa.

303
00:13:31,090 --> 00:13:33,830
Where's your final destination and what's the purpose of your trip to the United States?

304
00:13:34,550 --> 00:13:35,910
I'm going to visit my brother.

305
00:13:36,670 --> 00:13:37,970
He's just had a baby.

306
00:13:38,550 --> 00:13:39,630
He lives in Minneapolis.

307
00:13:40,760 --> 00:13:43,060
And how long do you plan to remain in the United States?

308
00:13:43,860 --> 00:13:46,180
I'll be here for approximately three weeks.

309
00:13:46,960 --> 00:13:50,140
So here's my return ticket for the 26th of March.

310
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:53,280
And who's sponsoring your trip?

311
00:13:54,250 --> 00:13:55,690
My brother here.

312
00:13:56,290 --> 00:13:57,890
This is an invitation letter from him.

313
00:13:58,350 --> 00:14:00,650
I will stay with him and his family in their home.

314
00:14:01,840 --> 00:14:03,240
All right.

315
00:14:03,240 --> 00:14:04,630
Tell me about the ties you have to your home country.

316
00:14:05,790 --> 00:14:07,190
Well, I own a house.

317
00:14:07,590 --> 00:14:09,690
Actually, I'm leaving my dog there with my neighbors.

318
00:14:10,470 --> 00:14:11,490
I have a car at home.

319
00:14:12,310 --> 00:14:13,090
I know my job.

320
00:14:13,850 --> 00:14:15,550
I'm employed by Tornell as an engineer.

321
00:14:16,240 --> 00:14:18,190
Actually, I only have three week vacation.

322
00:14:18,920 --> 00:14:20,850
So I have to go back to work at the end of March.

323
00:14:21,960 --> 00:14:24,290
And what evidence do you have that you are financially independent?

324
00:14:25,250 --> 00:14:27,170
Well, I do have assets in my country.

325
00:14:27,770 --> 00:14:34,550
Like I said, I own a house and see, here's a bank statement showing my investments and my bank balance.

326
00:14:35,860 --> 00:14:36,780
I'm sorry, sir.

327
00:14:36,980 --> 00:14:38,800
We cannot grant you a B2 visa at this time.

328
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:40,700
Instead, you are granted a resident visa.

329
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:41,280
Congratulations.

330
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:44,120
You are the millionth person to apply for a visa.

331
00:14:44,720 --> 00:14:45,020
You win.

332
00:14:45,540 --> 00:14:45,880
Congratulations.

333
00:14:54,000 --> 00:15:01,100
Well, I hope this was a useful lesson for all of our listeners because I'm sure that in one point or another, in the future, you're going to apply for a visa.

334
00:15:02,480 --> 00:15:08,000
So now you know all the language that you need to more or less answer all these questions that you're going to be asked.

335
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:09,000
Yes.

336
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:12,660
And I'm sure that people have already applied for visas for the US, right?

337
00:15:13,120 --> 00:15:13,740
Probably, yeah.

338
00:15:14,120 --> 00:15:18,600
And I want to hear from our users any stories that they have about applying for a visa.

339
00:15:19,020 --> 00:15:19,700
Yeah, that would be great.

340
00:15:20,080 --> 00:15:22,700
I know that I have some stories to share on the comments board.

341
00:15:23,840 --> 00:15:27,090
And we want to hear your questions and comments and any suggestions.

342
00:15:27,920 --> 00:15:33,850
So visit our website at EnglishPod.com and Marco and I will be there to respond to you and answer your questions.

343
00:15:34,510 --> 00:15:37,110
Well, everyone, thanks for listening and until next time.

344
00:15:37,690 --> 00:15:37,950
Bye.
